## KEYWORDS
graphene quantum dots, ammonium, edge-functionalization, cathode interlayer, polymer solar cells
## ABSTRACT
## 1 Introduction
The development of polymer solar cells (PSCs) is a
promising photovoltaic technology because of their potential applications in flexible, large-area, and light-weight devices with solution processing [1–5].
As the interfaces between the anode/cathode and the active layer are important for PSC device efficiency and stability, significant efforts have been devoted to the development of suitable anode interlayers (AILs) and cathode interlayers (CILs) [6, 7]. Many inorganic semiconductors such as \( \mathrm{ZnO} \) and \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) have been used as CILs and exhibit good device performance [8–11]. However, owing to the intrinsic incompatibility of the inorganic and organic materials, inorganic CILs suffer from poor interfacial contact with the organic active layer, leading to poor electron extraction. Solution-processed organic/polymer CILs demonstrate advantages of good contact with the active layer and chemical flexibility [12–29]. Organic/polymer CIL materials typically possess specific polar/ionic groups (amino, ammonium, phosphonate, zwitterions, etc.) attached to \( \pi \)-conjugated skeletons [12]. As most of the \( \pi \)-conjugated skeletons have poor electron mobilities/conductivities, these organic/polymer CIL materials can only work well when a very thin film is used [12–22]. Consequently, they are not compatible with large-area roll-to-roll processing of PSCs where thickness variations are unavoidable. Recently, thickness-insensitive organic/polymer CIL materials have been developed by using n-type \( \pi \)-conjugated skeletons with high electron mobilities/conductivities [23–29]. However, all these n-type \( \pi \)-conjugated skeletons show strong light absorption in the visible/near-infrared (NIR) region, which interferes with the sunlight absorption by the active layer and thus deteriorates device efficiency. Therefore, it is quite challenging to develop CILs with both excellent electron transporting properties and high transparency in the visible/NIR region.
Solution-processable graphene, including graphene oxide (GO) derivatives and graphene quantum dot (GQD) derivatives, are a promising class of AIL/CIL materials for PSCs [30]. The chemical structures of these graphene derivatives can be modified and their properties can be tuned to fulfill the requirements of AILs/CILs [30–46]. However, thickness-insensitive AILs/ CILs based on solution-processable graphene have never been reported. This prevents the practical application of graphene materials in large-area roll-to-roll processed PSCs. We have recently developed edge-functionalized GQDs as excellent AIL/CIL materials [45, 46]. In this study, we report a rationally designed thickness-insensitive
CIL based on GQDs with both good conductivity and high optical transparency. Figure 1(a) shows the schematic illustration of the chemical structure of the CIL material, GQDs functionalized with ammonium iodide at the edge (GQD-NI). GQD-NI was designed based on the following considerations. (i) GQD-NI can effectively lower the work function of cathode because the periphery ammonium iodide groups can form the desired interfacial dipole with the cathode [12]. (ii) In view of the high electron mobility and high conductivity of the graphene structure [47, 48], the graphene basal planes of GQD-NI lead to sufficiently good conductivity for thickness-insensitive CIL application (Fig. 1(c)). (iii) The small lateral size of \( \sim 3 \, \mathrm{nm} \) of the graphene basal plane of GQD-NI leads to large bandgap [49]. Therefore, GQD-NI does not absorb light in the visible/NIR region (Fig. 1(b)), which minimizes optical loss by the CIL. (iv) The small lateral size and peripheral ionic groups of GQD-NI lead to good solubility in polar organic solvents such as methanol and acetone. The orthogonal solvent processability of GQD-NI is necessary for fabricating multilayer devices via solution processing. As a result, using a blend of poly[N-9-hepta-decanyl-2,7-carbazolealt-5,5-(4,7-di-thienyl-2,1,3-benzothiadiazole)] (PCDTBT) and [6,6]-phenyl \( \mathrm{C}_{71} \)-butyric acid methyl ester \( (\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}) \) as the active layer (Fig. 1(a)) [50], we observed power conversion efficiency (PCE) enhancement from \( 5.38\% \) for an unmodified device to \( 7.49\% \) for the device incorporating the GQD-NI interlayer. Moreover, the device performance was insensitive to the GQD-NI layer thickness. With the GQD-NI interlayer thickness ranging from ca. 2 to \( 22 \, \mathrm{nm} \), the PCE was maintained at a value higher than \( 6\% \). These results indicated that GQD-NI is a promising material for the CIL in PSCs and can be potentially used in printed large-area devices that require wide processing windows.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis of GQDs
GQDs were synthesized as described in our previous report [41]. Briefly, a mixture of carbon black \( (2.5 \, \mathrm{g} \), Vulcan XC-72 carbon black, Cabot Corporation), concentrated \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4} \) (100 mL), and \( \mathrm{HNO}_{3} \) (50 mL) was stirred at \( 130 \, ^{\circ} \mathrm{C} \) for \( 24 \, \mathrm{h} \). Subsequently, the reaction
The mixture was cooled, diluted with de-ionized water, and neutralized with NaOH. The resulting mixture was then poured into acetone and the precipitate formed was removed by filtration. Evaporation of the solvent from the residual solution on a rotary evaporator yielded the desired GQDs.
## 2.2 Synthesis of GQD-N
A mixture of GQDs \((400~\mathrm{mg})\), 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) carbodiimide hydrochloride \((800\;\mathrm{mg})\), \(N\!\cdot\) -hydroxysulfosuccinimide \((400\,\mathrm{mg})\), and water \((200\,\mathrm{mL})\) was stirred for \(40\,\mathrm{\min}\). Next, 3-(dimethylamino)-1- propylamine \((200\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) was added to the mixture, followed by vigorous stirring at room temperature for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\). After workup, the mixture was extracted with dichloromethane and the organic phase was evaporated using a rotary evaporator to obtain a white solid. The solid was stirred with methanol and the insoluble part was removed by filtration. The filtrate was rotary evaporated to obtain GQD-N.
## 2.3 Synthesis of GQD-NI
The quaternization of GQD-N was carried out by stirring the solution of GQD-N \((360\;\mathrm{mg})\) with ethyl iodide
\((6\,\mathrm{mL})\) in ethanol \((180\,\mathrm{mL})\) in the dark at \(60\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Evaporation of the excess ethyl iodide and ethanol afforded a yellow solid, which was further dried under vacuum at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) to give GQD-NI.
## 2.4 PSC device fabrication and measurement
PCDTBT was synthesized according to our previous report [50]. \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and poly-[4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)thiophen-2-yl)benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b']dithiophene-co-3- fluorothieno[3,4-b]thiophene-2-carboxylate] (PTB7-Th) were respectively purchased from American Dye Source and 1-material Chemschein Inc. and used as received. Indium tin oxide (ITO) glass substrates were cleaned by ultra-sonication for \(10\,\mathrm{{min}}\) each sequentially with detergent, de-ionized water, acetone, and isopropanol, followed by drying at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{{h}}\) and treatment with UV-ozone for \(25\;\mathrm{min}\). A solution of poly(3,4-ethylene dioxythiophene)poly(styrene sulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) (Clevios VP Al 4083 from H. C. Starck Inc.) was spin-coated on the ITO substrates at \(5,000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(40\,\mathrm{s}\) and the assembly was annealed at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(1\,\mathrm{~h~}\). The substrates were transferred to a nitrogen-filled glove box. The PCDTBT: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) (1:4 by weight) active layer was spin-coated from the
\(o\)-dichlorobenzene (in total \(17.5\;\mathrm{mg\cdot mL^{-1}}\)) solution at \(1{,}200\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{min}\). The PTB7-Th:PC\(_{7}\)BM (1:1.5 by weight) active layer was spin-coated from the \(o\)-dichlorobenzene:1, 8-diiodooctane (97:3 volume ratio) solution at \(900\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{min}\). The GQD-NI layer was deposited on top of the active layer by spin-coating with the solution of GQD-NI in methanol at \(3{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\,\mathrm{s}\). Solution with different concentrations of GQD-NI were used to obtain GQD-NI interlayers with varying thickness. The substrates were then transferred to a vacuum chamber and Al \((100\;\mathrm{nm})\) was deposited on top of the active layer and the CIL under the vacuum of \(2\,\times\,10^{-4}\,\mathrm{Pa}\). The active area of each device was \(8.0\,\mathrm{mm}^{2}\). The current density–voltage \((J{-}V)\) curves of the devices under \(100\,\mathrm{mW\cdot cm^{-2}}\) AM1.5C simulated solar light illumination (XES-40S2-CE Class Solar Simulator, Japan, SAN-EI Electric Co., Ltd.) were recorded using a Keithley 2400 source meter. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra were recorded using the solar cell
spectral response measurement system QE-R3011 (Enlitech Co., Ltd.). The GQD-NI interlayer thickness was measured with the XP-plus stylus profilemeter together with the UV/Vis absorption spectra of the GQD-NI film. The conductivity of GQD-NI was calculated from the bulk resistance of the sample using Ohm's law \((R=V/I)\) with the device structure of ITO/GQD-NI \((40\,\mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{Al}\).
## 3 Results and discussion
the COOH groups of GQDs and 3-(dimethylamino)-1-propylamine (DAPA) afforded dimethylamino-functionalized GQDs. Finally, GQD-NI was prepared by quaternization of the dimethylamino-functionalized GQDs with excess ethyl iodide. GQD-NI exhibited good solubility \((>10\;\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) in water and polar organic solvents such as methanol and acetone. The solubility of GQD-NI indicated that it could be used to fabricate multifilayer PSC devices with orthogonal solvent processing.
According to the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) survey spectrum, the contents of nitrogen and iodine in GQD-NI were \(7.18\%\) and \(3.33\%\) (Fig. 2(b) and Table S1 in the ESM), respectively. In the XPS C1s spectrum (Fig. 2(c)), the band at \(285.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to the C–N groups also confirmed the presence of nitrogen. The TEM image shown in Fig. 2(d) suggested that the GQD-NI had a uniform lateral size of ca. \(3\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S2 in the ESM). The size of the GQD-NI was similar to that of the GQDs, indicating that the amidation and quaternization took place at the edge of the GQDs and did not affect the graphene basal plane. The small lateral size of the GQD-NI was responsible for its good solubility and important for its application in solution-processed opto-electronic devices. The thickness of the GQD-NI determined from AFM height image (Fig. 2(e)) was ca. \(0.5\,\mathrm{nm}\), which indicated a single-layered graphene structure.
Figure 1(b) shows the UV/Vis absorption spectrum of GQD-NI in aqueous solution. GQD-NI exhibited an absorption peak at \(228\,\mathrm{nm}\) with negligible absorbance in the visible and NIR range (Fig. S3 in the ESM). The shoulder observed in the spectrum at \(\sim350\,\mathrm{nm}\) was attributed to the amide group at the edge of the GQD-NI. As shown in the inset of Fig. 1(b), a methanol solution of GQD-NI with a concentration of \(1\,\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) appeared nearly colorless. The transparency of GQD-NI is exceedingly desirable for its application as a CIL in PSCs because it minimized the photon loss by the CIL and maximized the light absorption by the active layer. The conductivity of GQD-NI was estimated using the current–voltage curves of the ITO/GQD-NI \((40\,\mathrm{nm})/\mathrm{Al}\) device (Fig. 1(c)). The conductivity of GQD-NI was \(3.56\times10^{6}\,\mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is about three orders of magnitude higher than that of the well-known CIL material poly[(9,9-bis(3′-(N,N-dimethylamino)-propyl)-2,7-fluorene)-alt-2,7-(9,9-dio
ctylfluorene)] (PFN) \((6.90\times10^{9}\,\mathrm{S}\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), polyfluorene bearing amino groups) [20, 28]. The good conductivity of GQD-NI was attributed to the undestroyed graphene structure of the basal plane. In addition, the graphene structure may have been self-doped with the ammonium groups, which would also contribute to the high conductivity of GQD-NI [24, 51]. As described later, the high conductivity of GQD-NI is expected to not only decrease the series resistance but also lead to its insensitivity to the CIL thickness of the resulting PSC devices.
A crucial requirement of CIL materials is the ability to form the desired interfacial dipole at the cathode interface to decrease the work function. To test the effect of GQD-NI on the cathode work function, ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was employed. Figure 3(a) shows the secondary electron cutoff region in the UPS of the Al and Ag deposited with a thin layer of GQD-NI. With the GQD-NI interlayer, the work function of Ag decreased from 4.8 to \(3.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) because of the formation of a dipole at the metal interface. The work function of the GQD-NI modified Al electrode was found to be as low as \(3.4\,\mathrm{eV}\), which allowed the formation of Ohmic contact between the cathode and the active layer. Moreover,
the low work function increased the built-in electrical field across the active layer, leading to improved charge extraction and reduced charge recombination. These results demonstrated that GQD-NI could be effectively used as a CIL for PSCs.
To investigate the interfacial modification capability of GQD-NI, we fabricated PSC devices with PCDTBT: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) as the active layer (Fig. S4 in the ESM). The device was configured as ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PCDTBT: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}/\) CIL/Al (see Fig. 1(a)). The GQD-NI layers were deposited by spin-coating with the solutions in methanol with various concentrations. For comparison, we also fabricated the control devices without CIL or with Ca as the CIL. The \(J{-}V\) curves of the devices under AM1.5G illumination are presented in Fig. 4(a) and the corresponding EQE spectra are shown in Fig. 4(b). The characteristics of the PSC devices are listed in Table 1. The device with the bare Al cathode exhibited an open-circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{oc}})\) of \(0.86\,\mathrm{V},\) short-circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of \(10.31\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\) and fill factor (FF) of 0.607 corresponding to a PCE of \(5.38\%\) . With GQD-NI as the CIL at the optimal thickness of \(10\,\mathrm{nm},\) the device had \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(0.93\;\mathrm{V},\) \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of \(10.98\;\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},\) and
FF of 0.734, leading to a PCE of \(7.49\%\) . This performance was also much better than that of the device with Ca as the CIL, which showed a PCE of \(6.72\%\) . The excellent device performance of GQD-NI was primarily attributed to the decreased work function of the cathode, which led to Ohmic contact with \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) and enhanced the built-in potential in the device. In addition, as shown in Fig. 5, the GQD-NI-based device exhibited significantly reduced dark current density under reversed bias in the \(J{-}V\) curves in dark. This suggested that GQD-NI possessed good hole-blocking capability and improved charge selectivity at the cathode interface. Therefore, the GQD-NI as CILs could effectively prevent charge recombination at the cathode interface and led to the improved FF and increased \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) . The \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) of all the PSC devices matched well with the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values obtained via integration of the corresponding EQE spectra with an error of less than \(5\%\) .
The dependence of the PSC device performance on CIL thickness was investigated by spin-coating the GQD-NI solutions of different concentrations to give different CIL thicknesses. The \(J{-}V\) curves of the devices are shown in Fig. 6 and the device characteristics are summarized in Table 2. With the GQD-NI layer thickness increasing from 2 to \(22\,\mathrm{nm},\) the PCE of the device remained above \(6\%\) . Moreover, the series resistances of the devices increased only slightly from 6.20 to \(7.43\,\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) . The CIL thickness insensitivity was attributed
to the good conductivity and high transparency of GQD-NI. These results indicated that GQD-NI is an excellent thickness-insensitive CIL material and is potentially useful for printed PSCs that unavoidably experience thickness variations.
To verify the versatility of GQD-NI as the CIL, we further fabricated PSC devices using PTB7-Th: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) as the active layer with different CILs. The \(J{-}V\) curves of the devices under AM1.5G illumination and the EQE spectra are shown in Fig. S5 in the ESM. The device characteristics are listed in Table S2 in the ESM. The device with GQD-NI as a CIL exhibited a PCE of \(8.07\%\) , which was higher than that of the devices without a CIL by \(26.3\%\) \((\mathrm{PCE}=6.23\%)\) and with Ca as CIL by \(14.3\%\) \((\mathrm{PCE}=7.06\%)\) . These results confirmed the excellent device performance of GQD-NI as a CIL.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, GQDs functionalized with ammonium iodide at the edge were synthesized and employed as a thickness-insensitive CIL for PSCs. The peripheral
ammonium iodide groups in GQD-NI formed the desired interfacial dipole with the cathode to decrease the work function. The graphene basal planes of GQD-NI with a size of ca. \(3\,\mathrm{nm}\) exhibited good conductivity and high transparency in the visible/NIR region. The PSC device with GQD-NI as the CIL and PCDTBT: \(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}\) as the active layer had a PCE of \(7.49\%\) , which was much higher than that of the devices without a CIL \((\mathrm{PCE}=5.38\%)\) and with Ca as the CIL \((\mathrm{PCE}=6.72\%)\) . Moreover, the PSC device performance of GQD-NI was insensitive to the thickness of the GQD-NI layer. These results suggested that GQD-NI possess several properties desirable for CIL application and that they are very promising for application in large-area printed PSCs with unavoidable thickness variations.
